ust  observed but I thought you might like  this because this is data that's only  about three days old and it's always fun  to see what scientists are actually  doing right now so that's what we have  on that okay so today we've watched  three kinds of nature's nanomachines  molecular motors in action we watched a  walking cargo motor that carries all  kinds of things on a highway made-up  tubulin and its name is Kai neeson we  watched an energy-producing motor that  spins around so we looked and we even  watched ourselves a molecule of f-18  epa's and we looked at a tracking motor  that unzips DNA and it's called a  helicase so we've seen three different  examples of things in nature that are  basically nanomachines all of them burn  ATP and most of them are actually made  out of the same sort of LEGO pieces that  they just have different shapes and  functions and I want to close by  thanking all the people who made this  possible my talk today benefited from  the help of a bunch of my colleagues  Kevin Keating was a grad student in  computational biology Sean Taylor who is  a graduate student in molecular  biophysics Margaret treas and Andrew  who are grad students in physics and we  have very nice friends who do a lot of  this kind of work at university of  illinois dr. TJ ha and dr. Yan Cheng law  who gave us great ideas for movies and  who also sent us some data but  especially today we want to thank the  Howard Hughes Medical Institute because  it is doing a wonderful job bringing  science to the public through animations  and other resources and this is their  website if you can manage to copy any of  that down and and please make use of all  the things that hhmi is doing so anyway  thanks for your attention and to our  organizers also
 

